# Slice 346 (the book :375, "внешний b получает результат"; colon-declares) and plan M11 5a (the author 2026-11-11):
# `R: merge(A; B)` declares the Structure R, and the receiver in its tail builds it. Declared again by `R: merge(B; A)`
# and by `R: A B`, R is one more occurrence each time: data\[0]R is the first result (its y), data\[1]R the second (its
# x), data\[2]R holds the atoms A and B (two fields); a bare R would be ambiguous. `S: S` declares S with the atom S
# inside, not expanded; declared again it is a second occurrence with one field -- no cycle.
A:
    int: x 1
end: A
B:
    int: y 2
end: B
R: merge(A; B)
int: first data\[0]R\y
R: merge(B; A)
int: second data\[1]R\x
R: A B
int: n length(data\[2]R)
S: S
S: S
int: m length(data\[1]S)
